ent estimators have been developed (Williams, 1992; Grathwohl et al., 2018; Tucker et al., 2017; Gu et al., 2016; Yin et al., 2019), however they often require multiple forward passes of the model per one estimate and their variance can be still prohibitive.\nSingle-sample and low variance estimators are of high practical interest in applications. Gumbel Softmax (Jang et al., 2017) dubbed as Concrete relaxation (Maddison et al., 2017)1 enable differ-\n1These two works have contemporaneously introduced the same relaxation, but the name Gumbel Softmax (GS) became more popular in the literature.\nUnder review as a conference paper at ICLR 2023\nentiability through discrete variables by relaxing them to real-valued variables with a distribution approximating the original discrete distribution. These estimators are biased, meaning that they are systematically off. The tightness of the relaxation is controlled by the temperature parameter \u03c4 > 0 and the bias can be reduced by choosing a smaller temperature. However, the variance of the estimator grows asO(1/\u03c4) (Shekhovtsov, 2021). The Gumbel-Rao (GR) estimator (Paulus et al., 2021) is a recent improvement of the popular Gumbel-Softmax Straight-Through (GS-ST) variant (Jang et al., 2017), which uses discrete samples on the forward pass but relaxed ones on the backward. GR can substantially reduce the variance of GS-ST. however it involves an intractable local integration in multiple variables, which is approximated by sampling. The experiments (Paulus et al., 2021) suggest that this estimator performs better at lower temperatures.\nInspired by the performance of GR at low temperatures, we analyze its behavior for temperatures close to and in the limit of the absolute zero. In the case of binary variables we give an asymptotic series expansion around \u03c4 = 0 and show that the zero temperature limit of GR matches DARN, an already known estimator by Gregor et al. (2014). In the general categorical case, the limit 